[; reference/shop/shop-money — the kind=library package.

   A library holds CODE and no authority. The distribution spec is explicit
   (N-DIST-2): a `kind=library` package MUST NOT declare `[needs]`, and
   installing one issues no grants — enabling is granting, and there is
   nothing here to enable. It is exactly what it looks like: shared
   functions, pinned by content hash into the closure of whatever requires
   them.

   The shop's features format money in more than one place, so it lives
   here rather than being copied into each. `orders` requires it; the
   install pipeline resolves the pin transitively and records it under
   [libraries] in the deployment document. ]

[; Both defs are EXERCISED by the packaging test, not merely published.
   They were not, at first: the original `format-money` called
   `[$format:decimal …]`, which does not exist — `cx-stdlib/format` offers
   canonical / pretty / compact / diff-friendly and no such verb. It sailed
   through seal + publish + install anyway, because the exports gate checks
   that each exported def is PRESENT in the packaged code, never that the
   code runs. A library can therefore be published broken. Worth knowing,
   and worth a test that calls the things it ships. ]

[; MONEY IS A DECIMAL, END TO END (#1017). `19.99` is not a float — a bare
   fixed-point fraction autotypes as `decimal` (cxdm.md, the 2b ruling), and
   floats spell themselves with an exponent (`1.999e1`), so the two kinds are
   telling you apart on sight. `total-of` declares `[returns decimal]` and
   every step below keeps it: `[*]` folds the exact family with digit
   arithmetic, and so does `[$sum]`. Nothing here rounds, and nothing bridges
   to f64 — `[cast]` is the only decimal↔float bridge there is.

   That invariant was BROKEN once, silently, and it is worth knowing how.
   `[$sum]` classified its operands by the shape of their runtime payload
   instead of by their kind; a decimal carries its base-10 image as a string,
   so every decimal fell through to the numeric-string arm and got f64-parsed.
   This library kept declaring `decimal` and started returning `4.448e1`.

   Two things let it pass. The declared `[returns]` contract is only checked
   under `--strict` (dev-strict / prod-silent, code.md §12.7.6), and no lane
   ran this reference strictly. And a float total LOOKED fine until float
   images became exponent-form — the kind was wrong long before it was
   visible. Declare the kind, and pin the kind: an image is a hint. ]

[?def format-money scope=public pure [returns string] ($amount::decimal $currency::string)
  [$concat $currency ' ' [$concat '' $amount]]]

[?def total-of scope=public pure [returns decimal] ($lines)
  [$sum [?for [in $l $lines] [yield [* $l@qty $l@unit]]]]]
